Subject: [PATCH] config: don't use an uninitialised entry when a backend's get fails

get_entry() passes the address of an uninitialised local to the backend's
`get` and, for any result other than GIT_ENOTFOUND, stores `&be->entry`
in `*out`. A backend that fails with an error, as the file backend does
when reloading a changed config fails (an include that became unreadable,
say), leaves `be` untouched, so `*out` points at garbage and the caller
frees through it.

Initialise `be` and only hand out its entry when there is one.
---
diff --git a/src/libgit2/config.c b/src/libgit2/config.c
--- a/src/libgit2/config.c
+++ b/src/libgit2/config.c
@@ -805,10 +805,12 @@
 		GIT_ASSERT(entry->instance && entry->instance->backend);
 
 		backend = entry->instance->backend;
+		be = NULL;
 		res = backend->get(backend, key, &be);
 
 		if (res != GIT_ENOTFOUND) {
-			*out = &be->entry;
+			/* `be` is only set when the backend found the entry */
+			*out = be ? &be->entry : NULL;
 			break;
 		}
 	}
